      "protocol": "opencode",
      "note": "Cognia never starts this server, so it cannot be sandboxed or torn down by us; capability-wise it is identical to the auto-spawn preset.",
      "capabilities": {}
    },
    "opencode-v2-preview": {
      "protocol": "opencode-v2",
      "note": "Preview service Cognia never starts or stops.",
      "capabilities": {}
    }
  },
  "capabilityNotes": {
    "thinking": "For an external agent this asks whether the HOST can set the thinking / reasoning level, which is the only question any surface acts on. An agent that merely streams thought chunks (every ACP agent) does not satisfy it -- there is nothing to configure. Codex takes it on its options channel and Pi has `set_thinking_level`, so those two are `native`. OpenCode expresses the same idea as variants -- named request overlays for one model, its docs' words -- so v2 is `equivalent`, not `native`: `opencode-v2-client.ts` sends the chosen id as the `variant` half of the `ModelRef` on `v2.session.switchModel`, which is the `provider/model#variant` reference OpenCode itself documents. v1 has no such field anywhere in its API, so effort there is only settable in the user's own opencode.json and the row stays `unsupported`.",
    "mcp": "Whether Cognia can attach an MCP server for a session. ACP carries them on `session/new`; Codex has no per-session parameter and reaches the same outcome through a per-thread config override (`equivalent`); the rest have no channel.",
    "session.resume": "Reopening a prior session. On ACP this is gated on the agent advertising stable `sessionCapabilities.resume`, with legacy `loadSession` as a fallback, so the row is `unknown` and the handshake answers it.",
    "compaction": "ACP has no compaction method: the only route is a `/compact` or `/compress` the agent chose to advertise, which is a per-session live fact. Pi and Codex have native methods.",
    "hooks.lifecycle": "No external protocol models Cognia's lifecycle hooks, so every row is `unknown`. Whether the hooks run is a HOST fact: the renderer wraps every external turn, the CLI does not."
  }
}
